    "Notes Updated": "5/2/2025",
    "Scouting Notes": "Starter w/ relief risks, but risks are dwindling\n It's an extremely athletic delivery despite his long levers, and it's been tuned up quite a bit this year. Could see it in spring even.\n Fastball is elite. High spin, w/ ride, plays really well at the top of the zone. 96-99, touches triple digits. \n Slider is more of a cutter these days, sitting around 92-94 and landing in the zone at a high rate.\n Curveball is a plus or better pitch these days. Mid 80's pitch with late, sharp drop.\n Changeup has made huge strides in 2025, a legit average or better pitch suddenly.\n Command has made real strides this year as well\n As confident in his ability to start games as I've ever been, I think he's on the doorstep of his debut.",
    "Notes Updated 2": "6/20/2023",
    "Scouting Notes 2": "Starter w/ relief risks\n Much like Eric Brown Jr., this is someone I was very high on in the 2022 draft.\n It's an extremely athletic delivery despite his long levers.\n Bendy arm and really nice looking arm action. I don't worry too much about his arm having injury issues. \n I believe a move to the pen will not be due to his arm action, but due to his command.\n Fastball is elite. High spin, w/ ride, plays really well at the top of the zone. 94-98, touches triple digits. \n Spin Rate on the fastball averaging 2600 RPMs in Carolina.\n Slider is a plus pitch as well. 83-87 (getting into the 90's at times) with about a foot of sweep and some drop as well.\n Curve is also looking to be a plus pitch in pro ball. Power curve that can be absolutely filthy at times.\n Breaking balls averaging 2600-2700 RPM.\n Changeup is the pitch that probably needs the most help, but flashes being a solid pitch and should be a decent enough 4th option for him. \n For the CH, he needs to be able to throw for strikes and be willing to throw it in general. Was not needed against JuCo bats, so he rarely used it.\n Command is the biggest concern. It's not great. But the Brewers have done a pretty good job of reigning guys like that in to the zone, even if in zone command isn't perfect (see: Ashby).\n Arguably the most projectable arm in the draft, and the Brewers are great for profile's like that.\n Huge potential, could absolutely be the best arm in the draft if everything breaks right.\n Healthy floor is probably close to a major league reliever, due to the pure stuff.\n Seeing the velo drop from near 100 early on in starts to only around 93-95 in the 4th inning even. Does validate some of the relief risks. Though Brewers are building him up slowly.\n Saw him in person, it's a violent but in control delivery. Does need to get a bit more consistant with the delivery.\n Fastball ride is ridiculous and has extension that would be in 100th percentile in MLB.\n His ceiling is legitimately the best pitcher in baseball. But there's still a huge gap between floor and ceiling.",
